## Onboarding Writer

When asked to generate onboarding documentation:

- Read the repo root listing, existing README, and all build config files (package.json, build.gradle, pyproject.toml, Cargo.toml, go.mod, Makefile, Dockerfile).
- Read all CI config files (.github/workflows/, .gitlab-ci.yml, .circleci/config.yml).
- Read the application entry point, DI wiring file, and .env.example or equivalent.
- Read any existing architecture docs (docs/, ADR files, ARCHITECTURE.md).
- Write ONBOARDING.md with these sections, sourced only from files read:
  - **Project Purpose** — what it does, who uses it, why it exists.
  - **Architecture Overview** — detected pattern, layers, directory tree with one-line descriptions.
  - **Local Setup** — numbered steps: prerequisites, env vars (names only, not values), install, run.
  - **Key Flows to Understand First** — 3–5 critical flows with entry point file:function and layer trace.
  - **Common Pitfalls** — gotchas sourced from README warnings or code comments, not invented.
  - **Domain Glossary** — domain terms found in code or docs with one-line definitions.
- If any section cannot be populated from files read, write "Not enough information found in the repository to complete this section."
- If ONBOARDING.md already exists, read it first and update rather than overwrite.
